WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.000
"Blue Bloods" on CBS has a "who's&nbsp;
who" of talent in the cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.360
not the least of which is TV legend Tom&nbsp;
Selleck as the family patriarch Frank Reagan.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.840
To one generation of viewers, Selleck may be&nbsp;
best known for his role on "Magnum, P.I.,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.320
but his presence on "Blue Bloods" means he's&nbsp;
the rare actor with multiple iconic television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.440
roles to his name. Many actors are lucky enough&nbsp;
to get just one, but then again, Selleck isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.000
like most actors, and you can tell that's the&nbsp;
case by the way his co-stars talk about him.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.320
Selleck may have years of acting experience&nbsp;
under his belt, far more than any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.360
performer on "Blue Bloods," but Donnie Wahlberg,&nbsp;
who plays Danny Reagan on the police procedural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.000
more than holds his own against him. The two&nbsp;
have had ample scenes together over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.160
so suffice to say, the two actors have had&nbsp;
numerous opportunities to get to know each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.640
other. As expected, Wahlberg has nothing&nbsp;
but kind things to say about his co-star.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.160
Considering Tom Selleck has decades&nbsp;
of experience in the industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.360
it should come as no surprise to hear&nbsp;
that he's a delight to work with.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.760
In an interview with Outsider, Donnie Wahlberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.840
let it be known that he adores working&nbsp;
alongside the veteran actor. He said,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.520
"Tom [Selleck] is a leader himself. He is a very&nbsp;
experienced, very smart guy. Very dedicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.800
much like myself. There are times&nbsp;
when we both see the hidden game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.960
and we both want the same thing but we&nbsp;
have very different ideas on how to do it."

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.720
It's a great sensation for an actor to go&nbsp;
up against someone who challenges them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.200
It prevents one from getting complacent, and&nbsp;
helps them strive to deliver another stellar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.000
performance. Wahlberg goes on to describe how&nbsp;
it's almost like a dance between the two, saying,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.400
"Knowing when to step up, knowing that this&nbsp;
is the time Tom could really use my input.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.080
Or knowing when to step back and knowing&nbsp;
this is the time I gotta trust Tom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.840
It is very truthful off-screen as it&nbsp;
is on-screen for Danny and Frank."

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.240
Part of the reason why Selleck and Wahlberg&nbsp;
get along so well is because Wahlberg sees a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.280
lot of his own father in Selleck's character.&nbsp;
In a 2013 interview with The Huffington Post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.800
Wahlberg spoke about what drew him&nbsp;
to "Blue Bloods" in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.720
As one of nine children, Wahlberg could&nbsp;
relate to his character Danny as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.880
a group of siblings who often squabble,&nbsp;
but always come together in the end.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.760
Wahlberg said,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.520
"'Blue Bloods' is a throwback show because&nbsp;
they don't make shows that focus on families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.160
like this anymore. [...] Everything's focused&nbsp;
on the unusual family. When I read the pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.880
the family dinner scene attracted me most. I've&nbsp;
felt the heat of my dad staring down at me and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.600
sister while we're arguing and that same heat's&nbsp;
there with Tom Selleck and Bridget [Moynahan]."&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.480
And it's not just the arguments&nbsp;
that remind Wahlberg of his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.360
family — it's the lighter moments too. He said,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.680
"Just like we tease Tom on-screen, that's&nbsp;
directly from my childhood with my dad. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.160
was a very tough guy and we loved trying to break&nbsp;
through that hard exterior and make him laugh."

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.560
Wahlberg and Selleck's relationship has deepened&nbsp;
in the eleven years since "Blue Bloods" first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.640
went on the air. In a recent interview&nbsp;
with Entertainment Tonight, which he gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.360
shortly after the death of his mother, Alma,&nbsp;
Wahlberg talked about his bond with Selleck.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.520
"I call him dad now, and he calls me son. I mean,&nbsp;
that's how we talk to each other off-camera.”

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.400
With a show like "Blue Bloods," it&nbsp;
only works if the actors playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.480
a family can believably come off that&nbsp;
way. Luckily for Wahlberg and Selleck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.000
it doesn't sound like they have to fake&nbsp;
the father-son dynamic too much – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.160
that's one of the many reasons why "Blue&nbsp;
Bloods" has connected so well with fans.

